Early studies in sarcasm detection predominantly leaned on textual cues. \citep{ref-1,ref-2,ref-3,ref-43}. 
With the rapid growth of multimodal content on social media platforms, researchers began to explore multimodal sarcasm detection\citep{ref-4}. 
Cai et al.  introduced the MMSD dataset and proposed a hierarchical fusion model to jointly integrate textual and visual information. 
Following this work, several studies attempted to incorporate multimodal representations and pretrained language models to improve sarcasm detection performance\citep{ref-5,ref-6}. 
For example, Hazarika et al. \citep{ref-5} proposed modality-invariant and modality-specific representation learning, while Rahman et al. \citep{ref-6} integrated multimodal signals into pretrained transformer models. 
More recent studies focus on modeling fine-grained cross-modal interactions between textual and visual elements\citep{ref-7,ref-8,ref-44}. 
Liang et al. \citep{ref-7} proposed a cross-modal graph convolutional network to explicitly model relationships between multimodal entities, while Qin et al. \citep{ref-8} used CLIP-based multi-view representations to improve multimodal sarcasm detection performance.

