Despite the promising outlook, current multimodal sarcasm detection methods still face three key challenges. First, most existing studies have difficulty capturing local misalignment and fine-grained conflicts between text and images, while sarcasm often comes from subtle inconsistencies at the local level. The cross-modal local correspondence itself is highly complex and asymmetric, which makes fine-grained alignment hard to model accurately. Previous work such as BLIP\citep{ref-15,ref-45} explicitly models the alignment between words and visual regions, showing that fine-grained cross-modal modeling is important for capturing complex semantics. However, how to build a unified and effective fine-grained interaction representation is still an open problem.
Second, existing methods like ViLBERT\citep{ref-16} and LXMERT\citep{ref-17} usually use dense fully-connected mechanisms to model cross-modal interactions, where all text and visual elements are considered together. Although this way can cover enough cross-modal relations, many interactions are redundant or weakly related in real tasks. The truly useful local interactions are often sparse\citep{ref-46}. These high-value but sparse cross-modal signals are easily overwhelmed by noise introduced by dense modeling, which interferes with the model’s ability to identify key incongruent information. 
Third, the complex relationships between cross-modal elements are not only reflected in one-to-one alignment, but also involve multiple dependencies among emotional expression, contextual information, and visual content. This makes sarcasm semantics show clear high-order relational characteristics. Such complex dependencies are difficult to model through simple pairwise matching or shallow fusion, and require a mechanism that can explicitly model structural relations and perform global reasoning. 

