First, the proposed \textbf{FGCA} is designed to model local fine-grained cross-modal correspondences more accurately.This module constructs a cross-modal interaction matrix to explicitly represent the fine-grained association strength between text words and visual regions, forming a unified cross-modal interaction representation. Based on this interaction prior, the model can capture potential local misalignment and implicit semantic conflicts, providing a reliable structural basis for the following ICM and RCR modules.

Second, To reduce the interference of noise on high-value yet sparse cross-modal interactions, we introduce \textbf{ICM} as a dedicated mining mechanism. Different from traditional dense modeling, ICM performs top-k selection within local neighborhoods based on the interaction matrix, and only keeps the most representative cross-modal interactions. On this basis, a dynamic routing mechanism is introduced to adaptively aggregate local information, strengthen key incongruity signals, and suppress irrelevant or noisy interactions. This allows the model to focus on cross-modal contradictions that are important for sarcasm detection.

Third, To better capture the structural complexity of multimodal sarcasm, we introduce \textbf{RCR} to explicitly reason over complex dependencies and high-order relations across modalities.This module builds a cross-modal bipartite graph based on the interaction matrix, organizing text nodes and visual nodes into a unified graph representation. Through multi-layer message passing, information exchange between nodes is achieved. At the same time, global semantic enhancement is introduced to supplement overall context information, enabling structured modeling and reasoning of high-order multimodal relations, and improving the model’s understanding of complex sarcasm semantics.

