\section{Related work}
\ljd{The whole section requires citations.}

\subsection{Multimodal Sarcasm Detection}

With the widespread dissemination of text and images on social media, sarcastic expressions increasingly exhibit cross-modal fusion characteristics, making it difficult for single-text analysis to fully capture their implicit semantics. Consequently, Multimodal Sarcasm Detection (MSD) has emerged as a significant research direction in the fields of natural language processing and multimodal learning in recent years\citep{ref-47,ref-48}.

Multimodal Sarcasm Detection (MSD) has continuously evolved on the MMSD\citep{ref-1} and MMSD2.0 \citep{ref-19} datasets, transitioning from early approaches centered on cross-modal incongruity through contrastive learning\citep{ref-18,ref-20,ref-21,ref-22,ref-26,ref-28}to recent works emphasizing fine-grained characterization of "semantic-pragmatic" aspects and context. The TCIP framework proposed by Gao et al. \citep{ref-24} enhances generalization on the MMSD series datasets by constructing unimodal graphs for text and vision, as well as a visual-text heterogeneous graph. It employs Graph Convolutional Network (GCN) and dynamic routing to jointly model intra-modal and cross-modal topologies, unifies visual-visual, textual-textual, and visual-textual relationships within the heterogeneous graph's association matrix, and further uses contrastive learning to pull together true matches while pushing away spurious correlations. Meanwhile, the DDMGF method by He et al. \citep{ref-23} decouples intra-modal and inter-modal sarcasm, proposing a bi-layer region-aware attention and dynamic multi-granularity fusion to systematically address issues of interference from irrelevant features and neglected intra-modal conflicts. 

 \subsection{Cross-Modal Alignment}
Cross-modal alignment strategies typically involve correspondence at a fine-grained level between words and image regions, while concurrently assessing the intensity of cross-modal semantic conflicts\citep{ref-49}. Wang et al. \citep{ref-7} combines CLIP with co-attention, performing shallow interactions before deep relational alignment to mitigate the complexity of graph construction and mismatches in dynamic context updates. The aforementioned TCIP framework complements this approach from the perspectives of graph topology and distributional opposition. For noisy and text-dense images, Dong et al. \citep{ref-32} inject affective semantics via OCR routing and BLIP-2 (LoRA), enabling cross-modal alignment to consider both "object" and "affective" dimensions. These practices, complemented by fine-grained alignment and consistency constraint methods used by Qin et al. \citep{ref-19} and Dai et al. \citep{ref-33}, reflecting an evolution of alignment objectives from "matching" to "calibrating conflicts" in a task-customized manner. 

\subsection{Attention-Based Fusion}

The evolution of attention-based fusion has concentrated on two threads: firstly, selectively aggregating key cues through cross-modal co-attention, such as Wen et al. \citep{ref-22} modeling on both factual and affective layers, Wang et al. \citep{ref-17} employing weight-sharing cross-attention, and He et al. \citep{ref-23} filtering irrelevant features and aligning key cues at multiple granularities via bi-layer region-aware attention; second, capturing internal sarcastic conflicts within text or images through intra-modal multi-head self-attention. Guan  et al. \citep{ref-34}, in a textual context, perceives incongruity with "multi-head self-matching + multi-head co-matching" and combines it with transfer learning, providing a reusable attention design and training paradigm for cross-modal tasks. Currently, contrastive learning is used in conjunction with \citep{ref-26}\citep{ref-27} to mitigate the risk of attention mistaking correlation for causality. 

\subsection{Graph-based Multimodal Reasoning}

The advantage of graph-based multimodal reasoning lies in its explicit modeling of relationships and higher-order interactions\citep{ref-50}. Liang et al. \citep{ref-25} constructs graphs from words to entity regions to delineate cross-modal sarcastic relationships, Zhuang et al. \citep{ref-31} introduces the KGGC-Net framework with a common sense knowledge graph to complete implicit cues, and Wei et al. \citep{ref-27} propose G2SAM to enhance global semantic awareness. The aforementioned TCIP framework integrates unimodal and heterogeneous graphs, combining dynamic routing with contrastive constraints for robust reasoning. At the same time, to overcome the bottlenecks of fixed adjacency matrices and the dependence on detector quality, recent work has been shifted towards data-driven graphs, knowledge enhancement, and joint training with contrastive learning \citep{ref-30,ref-31,ref-32}. 
 
 




