\subsection{Overall Architecture}
As shown in Figure ~\ref{fig2}, the proposed model jointly models multimodal sarcasm from textual, image, and cross-modal interaction perspectives. Given input text and images, the model first utilizes a pre-trained CLIP backbone to extract token-level features from text and patch-level features from images, mapping them to a unified semantic space. Simultaneously, global features from text and images are used to construct separate text-based and image-based classification branches, capturing sarcasm cues at the unimodal level.

FGCA constructs an explicit cross-modal interaction matrix from textual and visual features to characterize token-patch-level correspondences. Based on this interaction matrix, the model designs two complementary enhancement paths. On the one hand, it applies dynamic routing to aggregate the top-$k$ key cross-modal associations in the interaction matrix, thereby highlighting cross-modal incongruity cues that are relevant to sarcasm detection while suppressing noise interference. On the other hand, it formulates the selected token-patch relations as a bipartite graph and performs relational reasoning with a graph neural network to capture higher-order cross-modal semantic contrasts and contradictory relations; the resulting output is then injected into the fused features through a gating mechanism.

Finally, the model generates predictions from the textual view, the visual view, and the cross-modal interaction view, and uses a fusion classification module to jointly integrate multi-view information and produce the final multimodal sarcasm detection result.


\subsection{FGCA }
To fully model the fine-grained semantic alignment between text and images, this paper proposes an interaction-matrix-driven cross-modal interaction module. Based on the pairwise similarities between text tokens and image patches, the module first constructs a unified cross-modal interaction representation, then performs contextual enhancement through bidirectional cross-attention, and finally aggregates the representations into a sentence-level and image-level fused feature representation.

Specifically, we use a pretrained CLIP model as the encoder. As a pretrained model based on contrastive learning, CLIP integrates visual and language components and maps image-text pairs into a shared feature space. The extraction of textual and visual embeddings can be formulated as:


\begin{equation}
T = \{t_1, t_2, \ldots, t_m\} = \mathrm{CLIP}(\mathrm{Text})
\end{equation}


\begin{equation}
V = \{v_1, v_2, \ldots, v_n\} = \mathrm{CLIP}(\mathrm{Image})
\end{equation}


Thus, we obtain the token-level textual representation $T = \{t_i\}_{i=1}^{N}$, where $t_i \in \mathbb{R}^{d}$, and the patch-level visual representation $V = \{v_j\}_{j=1}^{M}$, where $v_j \in \mathbb{R}^{d}$. Here, $T \in \mathbb{R}^{m \times d}$ and $V \in \mathbb{R}^{n \times d}$ denote the embedding matrices of the text and image, respectively. In particular, $t_i \in \mathbb{R}^{d}$ is the textual embedding of the $i$-th word, and $v_j \in \mathbb{R}^{d}$ is the visual embedding of the $j$-th image patch. The variable $d$ denotes the embedding dimension.

Next, we construct a fine-grained cross-modal interaction score matrix between text and vision to measure the local semantic correspondences between the two modalities. The specific operation is as follows:

To reduce the distributional discrepancy between features from different modalities and improve cross-modal comparability, we first apply linear projection and normalization to the textual and visual features, respectively:


\begin{equation}
\tilde{t}_i = \mathrm{LN}(\mathrm{Norm}(W_t t_i)), \qquad
\tilde{v}_j = \mathrm{LN}(\mathrm{Norm}(W_v v_j))
\end{equation}


where $W_t, W_v \in \mathbb{R}^{d \times d}$ are learnable projection matrices.

In the unified embedding space, the interaction strength between any text token and image patch is computed using scaled dot-product similarity, yielding the cross-modal interaction score matrix:


\begin{equation}
E_{i,j} = \alpha \cdot \tilde{t}_i^{\top}\tilde{v}_j, \qquad
E \in \mathbb{R}^{N \times M}
\end{equation}


where

\begin{equation}
\alpha = \exp(\lambda)
\end{equation}


is a learnable temperature coefficient used to control the smoothness of the similarity distribution. A larger $\alpha$ amplifies high-confidence matches, thereby highlighting key cross-modal alignments.

The resulting matrix $E$ explicitly characterizes the semantic relevance between text tokens and image patches. Each element $E_{i,j}$ indicates the degree of semantic matching between the $i$-th text token and the $j$-th image patch in the shared semantic space.

It is worth noting that the interaction matrix $E$ is passed as a shared structural prior to the following two parallel enhancement paths. Specifically, the ICM path performs top-$k$ neighborhood selection and dynamic routing enhancement based on $E$ to model locally high-correlated cross-modal interactions. In contrast, the RCR path constructs a cross-modal bipartite graph based on $E$ and conducts reasoning over cross-modal relations through structured message passing.


In addition to the fine-grained cross-modal interaction representation, this paper also preserves the global semantic representations of the text modality and the image modality for auxiliary discrimination in the subsequent prediction stage. Specifically, after being processed by the CLIP text encoder, the input text yields a global pooled representation $\bar{t}$, which is then passed through a linear mapping to obtain the unimodal textual representation:

\begin{equation}
h^{\text{text}} = f_{\text{text}}(\bar{t})
\end{equation}


where $f_{\text{text}}(\cdot)$ denotes the linear mapping function on the text side.

Accordingly, $h^{\text{text}}$ and $h^{\text{image}}$ represent the unimodal global semantic features of the text modality and the image modality, respectively, and are used in the subsequent prediction stage. In contrast, the context-enhanced representations $c_i^t$ and $c_i^v$ derived from the interaction matrix $E$, together with the subsequent ICM and RCR modules, focus on fine-grained cross-modal interaction and relational reasoning. These two types of representations are functionally complementary and jointly support the final multi-head prediction stage.

\subsection{ICM }

Building on the cross-modal interaction matrix $E$ constructed by FGCA, the ICM path further uses $E$ to perform top-$k$ sparse neighborhood selection for each text token and image patch, and reinforces fine-grained matching relationships within locally highly correlated cross-modal neighborhoods through a dynamic routing mechanism.

\subsubsection{Dynamic Routing-Based Cross-Modal Enhancement}

We first project the cross-modal features into a shared representation space. Specifically, for each text token $t_i$, we select its top-$k$ most relevant image features and compute their transformed representations:


\begin{equation}
\tilde{v}_{i,r} = W_v v_r,\quad r \in N_k(i)
\end{equation}


where $W_v \in \mathbb{R}^{d \times d}$ is a learnable linear projection matrix, and $N_k(i)$ denotes the set of the top-$k$ most relevant visual neighbors of the text feature $t_i$.

To model fine-grained cross-modal interactions, we adopt a dynamic routing mechanism. The routing process iteratively updates the coupling coefficients based on feature consistency. We initialize the routing logits $b_{i,r}^{(0)}$ with the cross-modal interaction scores $E_{i,r}$:


\begin{equation}
b_{i,r}^{(0)} = E_{i,r}
\end{equation}


At each iteration, the coupling coefficients are computed as follows:


\begin{equation}
c_{i,r} = \frac{\exp\left(b_{i,r}^{(q)}\right)}{\sum_{r=1}^{k} \exp\left(b_{i,r}^{(q)}\right)}
\end{equation}


where $b_{i,r}^{(q)}$ denotes the routing logit at the $q$-th iteration. Then, the aggregated representation for each text token is given by:


\begin{equation}
s_i = \sum_{r=1}^{k} c_{i,r}\tilde{v}_{i,r}
\end{equation}


The aggregated vector is then passed through a squash function:


\begin{equation}
t_i^{\text{route}} = \frac{\|s_i\|^2}{1+\|s_i\|^2}\cdot\frac{s_i}{\|s_i\|}
\end{equation}


This function preserves the vector direction while constraining the magnitude of the output vector.

The routing logits are further updated based on the agreement between the projected features and the routing output:


\begin{equation}
b_{i,r}^{(q+1)} \leftarrow b_{i,r}^{(q)} + \tilde{v}_{i,r}^{\top} t_i^{\text{route}}
\end{equation}


The above routing process is repeated for $Q$ iterations to progressively refine cross-modal alignment.

Finally, the routed features are fused with the original representations through a residual connection and a feed-forward network:


\begin{equation}
t_i^{\text{icm}} = \mathrm{LN}\left(t_i + \mathrm{FFN}\left(t_i^{\text{route}}\right)\right)
\end{equation}


The image-side features can be updated in a symmetric manner.

\subsubsection{ICM Path Readout}

Accordingly, we obtain the enhanced representations on the text side and the image side, denoted by $\{t_i^{\text{icm}}\}$ and $\{v_j^{\text{icm}}\}$, respectively. We then further extract representative nodes from the two sequences, denoted by $h_t^{\text{icm}}$ and $h_v^{\text{icm}}$. Next, shared linear scoring followed by normalization is applied to the two representations to obtain modality-level fusion weights:


\begin{equation}
[\beta_t, \beta_v] = \mathrm{softmax}([a(h_t^{\text{icm}}), a(h_v^{\text{icm}})])
\end{equation}


where $a(\cdot)$ denotes the scoring function. Based on the above weights, the fused representation can be obtained as:


\begin{equation}
h^{\text{icm}} = \beta_t h_t^{\text{icm}} + \beta_v h_v^{\text{icm}}
\end{equation}


\subsection{RCR}

Similarly, based on the interaction matrix $E$ provided by FGCA, the RCR path constructs top-$k$ sparse edges according to cross-modal relevance, organizes text tokens and image patches into a cross-modal bipartite graph, and performs reasoning over inter-node relationships through structured message passing.

\subsubsection{Bidirectional Cross-Modal Message Passing}

To emphasize strongly correlated cross-modal alignment relationships while reducing the noise and computational cost introduced by full connectivity, we construct a bipartite graph between text nodes and image nodes based on the interaction matrix $E$, where edges are defined by similarity. Only the top-$k$ most relevant nodes are retained as adjacency relations, thereby forming a sparse graph structure. For each text token $t_i$, we select its $k$ most relevant image patches on the image side as its neighborhood $N_k(i)$. Similarly, for each image patch $v_j$, we select its $k$ most relevant text tokens on $E^\top$ as its neighborhood $N_k(j)$.

(1) Text-Side Aggregation

For each text token $t_i$, we normalize the interaction scores over its neighborhood $N_k(i)$ to obtain the cross-modal attention weights:


\begin{equation}
\alpha_{i,r} = \frac{\exp(E_{i,r})}{\sum_{p \in N_k(i)} \exp(E_{i,p})}, \quad r \in N_k(i)
\end{equation}


Then, the image patch representations within the neighborhood are linearly transformed and aggregated with attention weights to obtain the information from the image side:


\begin{equation}
m_i^v = \sum_{r \in N_k(i)} \alpha_{i,r} W_v v_r
\end{equation}


where $W_v \in \mathbb{R}^{d \times d}$ is a learnable linear projection matrix.

(2) Image-Side Aggregation

Similarly, for each image patch $v_j$, we normalize the interaction scores over its neighborhood $N_k(j)$ to obtain the weights:


\begin{equation}
\beta_{j,q} = \frac{\exp(E_{q,j})}{\sum_{p \in N_k(j)} \exp(E_{p,j})}, \quad q \in N_k(j)
\end{equation}


Then, the text-side information is aggregated as:


\begin{equation}
m_j^t = \sum_{q \in N_k(j)} \beta_{j,q} W_t t_q
\end{equation}


During training, the neighborhood edge weights can be randomly dropped and then renormalized to improve robustness.

\subsubsection{Node Update and Global Semantic Enhancement}

After obtaining the bidirectional messages, we update the node states using a Transformer-style formulation of "feed-forward network + residual connection + normalization":


\begin{equation}
t_i = \mathrm{LN}\left(t_i + \mathrm{FFN}_t(m_i^v)\right)
\end{equation}


\begin{equation}
v_j = \mathrm{LN}\left(v_j + \mathrm{FFN}_t(m_j^t)\right)
\end{equation}


The above process is stacked for $L$ layers to achieve multi-step cross-modal relational reasoning and information propagation, thereby yielding the inferred node sequence representations $\{t_i^{(L)}\}$ and $\{v_j^{(L)}\}$.

To incorporate sample-level global semantics and alleviate the limitation of relying only on local neighborhoods, RCR can further introduce a global state $g$. The global state in the first layer is initialized by the global aggregation results of the text and image nodes:


\begin{equation}
g^{(0)} = W_g \left(
\left[
\frac{\sum_{i=1}^{m} M_i \cdot t_i}{\sum_{i=1}^{m} M_i},
\frac{1}{n}\sum_{j=1}^{n} v_j
\right]
\right)
\end{equation}


where $M_i$ denotes the validity mask at the $i$-th position of the text sequence.

The global input is obtained by aggregating information from the text side and the image side, and is then updated iteratively as:


\begin{equation}
g^{(l)} = \mathrm{LN}\left(g^{(l-1)} + \mathrm{FFN}_g\left(g^{\mathrm{in},(l)}\right)\right)
\end{equation}


Meanwhile, the information in $g$ is injected into the text and image messages through linear mapping, allowing global semantics to modulate local interactions.


\subsubsection{RCR Path Readout}

After the reasoning process is completed, we extract representative nodes from the final updated text node sequence and image node sequence. Specifically, on the text side, we select the representation of the last valid token, denoted by $h_t^{\text{rcr}}$, and on the image side, we select the representation of the zeroth global image patch, denoted by $h_v^{\text{rcr}}$. Then, a shared linear scoring function is used to score the two representations, and modality-level weights are obtained through softmax normalization:


\begin{equation}
[\gamma_t, \gamma_v] = \mathrm{softmax}([a(h_t^{\text{rcr}}), a(h_v^{\text{rcr}})])
\end{equation}


Based on these weights, the local relational representation is obtained as:


\begin{equation}
h_{\text{local}}^{\text{rcr}} = \gamma_t h_t^{\text{rcr}} + \gamma_v h_v^{\text{rcr}}
\end{equation}


Furthermore, to preserve sample-level global semantic information, $h_{\text{local}}^{\text{rcr}}$ is concatenated with the global state $g$ and projected into a unified semantic space through $W_o$, yielding the final RCR representation $h^{\text{rcr}}$:


\begin{equation}
h^{\text{rcr}} = W_o [h_{\text{local}}^{\text{rcr}}, g]
\end{equation}


\subsection{Fusion and Prediction}
\subsubsection{Parallel Path Fusion}

In the preceding sections, we obtained the fused representation $h^{\text{icm}}$ from the ICM path and the reasoning representation $h^{\text{rcr}}$ from the RCR path. Since the two paths focus on complementary cross-modal information, we fuse them before prediction to obtain a more effective cross-modal discriminative representation.

Considering that the contribution of the RCR path to the final decision may vary across samples, we first introduce a gating mechanism to adaptively regulate the injection strength of the RCR branch. Specifically, the representations from the two paths are concatenated and fed into a gating function to obtain the fusion coefficient:


\begin{equation}
\mu = \sigma \left( \theta + \omega^{\top}[h^{\text{icm}}, h^{\text{rcr}}] \right) \cdot \rho
\end{equation}


where $\sigma(\cdot)$ denotes the Sigmoid function, $\theta$ and $\omega$ are learnable parameters, and $\rho$ is a scaling factor used to control the injection strength of the RCR branch.

Then, the RCR-path representation is scaled by this gating coefficient to obtain the gated reasoning representation:


\begin{equation}
\hat{h}^{\text{rcr}} = \mu h^{\text{rcr}}
\end{equation}


Before prediction, the two representations are concatenated and mapped linearly to obtain the final cross-modal fused representation:


\begin{equation}
h^{\text{final}} = W_f [h^{\text{icm}}, \hat{h}^{\text{rcr}}]
\end{equation}


where $W_f$ is a learnable fusion projection matrix. This representation integrates the fine-grained cross-modal interaction information from the ICM path and the structured relational reasoning information from the RCR path, and serves as the core input for subsequent classification prediction.

\subsubsection{Multi-Head Prediction and Optimization Objective}

To fully exploit the fused representation and the unimodal information, this paper designs three prediction branches in the prediction stage: a fusion head, a text head, and an image head. Specifically, based on the final fused representation $h^{\text{final}}$, the unimodal textual representation $h^{\text{text}}$, and the unimodal visual representation $h^{\text{image}}$, the corresponding classification logits are computed as follows:


\begin{equation}
z_f = W_f^{\text{cls}} h^{\text{final}} + b_f
\end{equation}


\begin{equation}
z_t = W_t^{\text{cls}} h^{\text{text}} + b_t
\end{equation}


\begin{equation}
z_v = W_v^{\text{cls}} h^{\text{image}} + b_v
\end{equation}


where $W_f^{\text{cls}}$, $W_t^{\text{cls}}$, and $W_v^{\text{cls}}$ denote the classification parameters of the fusion head, the text head, and the image head, respectively. The terms $b_f$, $b_t$, and $b_v$ are the learnable bias vectors of the classifiers corresponding to the fused representation, the textual representation, and the visual representation, respectively. They are used to enhance the affine transformation capability of the linear classifiers and improve class discrimination.

Furthermore, softmax is applied to convert the logits of the three classification heads into class probabilities:


\begin{equation}
p_f = \mathrm{softmax}(z_f)
\end{equation}


\begin{equation}
p_t = \mathrm{softmax}(z_t)
\end{equation}


\begin{equation}
p_v = \mathrm{softmax}(z_v)
\end{equation}


To integrate the three results, this paper performs joint decision-making over the outputs of the three heads. Let


\begin{equation}
P = [p_f, p_t, p_v]
\end{equation}


Then, the final class probability is given by:


\begin{equation}
p = \sum_{m \in \{f,t,v\}} \omega_m \odot p_m
\end{equation}


where $\omega_m$ denotes the fusion weight corresponding to the $m$-th classification head. It adaptively adjusts the contribution of each head according to its reliability for the current class, thereby improving the robustness of the final prediction.

During training, all three classification heads are jointly supervised, and the overall classification loss is defined as:


\begin{equation}
\mathcal{L}_{\text{cls}} = \sum_{m \in \{f,t,v\}} \omega_m \mathcal{L}_m
\end{equation}



\begin{equation}
\mathcal{L}_m = \mathrm{CE}(z_m, y)
\end{equation}


where $\mathrm{CE}(\cdot)$ denotes the cross-entropy loss.

Finally, the model outputs the predicted class based on the joint predictive distribution $p$.

\section{Experiments}
\subsection{Experimental Setup}
We conducted experiments on two public datasets: MMSD 1.0 and MMSD 2.0. Both datasets originate from image-text posts published by Twitter (now X) users. Each sample consists of an image paired with corresponding text, annotated as sarcastic or non-sarcastic, as detailed in Table ~\ref{tab1}. MMSD 1.0 leans toward samples with explicit cross-modal conflicts, demanding strong cross-modal semantic alignment capabilities from models. MMSD 2.0 is an upgraded version derived from MMSD 1.0 through expansion and data cleaning. It features stricter annotations and incorporates more complex forms of sarcastic expression.

All experiments were conducted on an NVIDIA GeForce RTX 4060 Laptop GPU using CUDA 11.7 and PyTorch 1.13.1 with the clip-vit-base-patch32 model. The optimizer employed Adam/AdamW with a learning rate of 4e-4 and a batch size of 32, utilizing cross-entropy loss for supervised learning.

Model performance was evaluated using Precision (P), Recall (R), Micro-F1 score, and Accuracy (Acc) as metrics.
