On the MMSD dataset, our model achieves 89.97\% accuracy and 87.33\% F1-score, demonstrating overall performance at the forefront of current mainstream approaches. Compared to traditional cross-modal attention models (e.g., Att-BERT, CMGCN), our method shows significant improvements in F1 metrics, indicating that the proposed fine-grained alignment and conflict modeling mechanism more effectively captures cross-modal semantic inconsistencies.

On the MMSD 2.0 dataset, our model achieved 88.25\% accuracy and 86.67\% F1-score, significantly outperforming most comparison methods and surpassing recent models like CofiiPara. Given that MMSD 2.0 contains more implicit sarcasm samples, this result further validates the model's robustness in complex cross-modal reasoning scenarios.

Compared to earlier fusion-based and attention-based models, our approach achieves significant improvements on both datasets, demonstrating the necessity of explicitly modeling cross-modal inconsistencies rather than relying solely on global attention mechanisms. Compared to graph-based reasoning models like CMGCN and G²SAM, our model demonstrates competitive or superior performance, indicating that introducing conflict-aware sparse mining prior to relational inference yields more informative and less noisy interaction structures. Even against recent state-of-the-art models like Tang et al. and CofiiPara, our approach achieves superior or comparable performance, particularly on the MMSD2.0 dataset, highlighting its effectiveness in handling subtle implicit sarcasm cases.

Notably, the improvement is more pronounced on the MMSD2.0 dataset compared to MMSD, likely due to the higher proportion of implicit sarcasm cases in MMSD2.0. The proposed conflict mining and relation inference mechanism excels at modeling subtle cross-modal inconsistencies. Furthermore, balanced performance in both precision and recall further demonstrates that the model does not exhibit over-bias toward any particular category.

\subsection{Ablation study}
To validate the effectiveness of each key module, we conducted ablation experiments by removing the \textbf{FGCA}, \textbf{ICM}, and \textbf{RCR} branches respectively on the MMSD and MMSD 2.0 datasets, with results shown in Table~\ref{tab3} . Overall, removing any single module leads to a significant performance drop, indicating that all three branches play crucial roles in the model.

To assess the contribution of the \textbf{FGCA} branch, we removed the fine-grained cross-modal alignment module and directly fed the global text and image representations extracted by the shared encoder into subsequent modules. Specifically, we discarded the word-level cross-modal attention mechanism and local semantic correspondence modeling, while \textbf{ICM} and \textbf{RCR} operated based on coarse-grained global features. Removing the \textbf{FGCA} branch caused significant performance drops on both datasets, particularly pronounced on the MMSD2.0 dataset. This indicates that without fine-grained alignment, downstream modules lack precise cross-modal semantic correspondences, weakening their discord modeling capabilities. Without the \textbf{ICM} module, the model's F1 score drops significantly, indicating that explicitly mining conflict signals is crucial for sarcasm detection. While recall remains high, precision decreases, suggesting that without conflict-aware filtering, the model is more prone to misclassifying non-sarcastic samples. Removing the \textbf{RCR} branch causes precision to decrease while recall increases, indicating that relational reasoning helps suppress noisy cross-modal interactions and reduce false positives.

Overall, \textbf{FGCA }facilitates fine-grained semantic alignment, \textbf{ICM} explicitly models cross-modal inconsistencies, and \textbf{RCR} enhances structural reasoning and noise suppression capabilities. The consistent degradation observed across all ablation experiments validates the necessity of the proposed multi-branch architecture.
