\section{Case study}

\subsection{Successful Cases}




To better illustrate the advantages of FIRM, we select four representative samples from the MMSD2.0 test set (Figure~\ref{fig:3}), all of which are correctly identified as sarcastic by our model but misclassified by both baselines, HKEmodel and Multi-view CLIP. 

Figure~\ref{fig:3-a} depicts a typical case of polarity reversal, in which a package that obviously cannot fit through the door is wedged into the gap, while the text describes the delivery as "nice and secure". Relying mainly on global image-text similarity, HKEmodel (0.22) and MV-CLIP (0.41) are misled by the literal positive sentiment. In contrast, FIRM aligns the word "secure" with local patches of the door gap and the deformed package at the token-patch level through FGCA, and ICM further amplifies this inconsistency, eventually leading to a confident prediction of 0.93. While Figure~\ref{fig:3-a} emphasizes a local visual anomaly, Figure~\ref{fig:3-b} reflects a more global spatial conflict: although the text claims that the venue has "huge performance areas", the band is squeezed into only a tiny corner of the image. The baselines, dominated by salient regions such as stage lighting and the crowd (HKE 0.28; MV-CLIP 0.39), fail to capture this contradiction, whereas FIRM uses the top-k selection of ICM to concentrate on the key conflict between the band's actual size and the textual claim, yielding a probability of 0.91.

The advantage of fine-grained cross-modal alignment becomes even more crucial when the visual signal lacks salient objects and the overall appearance is highly uniform. Figure~\ref{fig:3-c} illustrates such a situation, where a platform is buried under thick snow and the image is dominated by a large white region, while the text reads "love this weather". Under this setting, HKEmodel (0.31) and MV-CLIP (0.44) can hardly extract the conflict from coarse global features, whereas FIRM, by aligning the sentiment word "love" with fine-grained patches of the snow-covered tracks via FGCA, correctly identifies the contrast between the text and the scene (0.89). Figure~\ref{fig:3-d} further introduces an implicit reasoning demand: products on the shelves have been damaged and scattered around, yet the text sarcastically states "I love respectful customers", whose ironic meaning relies on the behavioral relation between customers and products. The baselines (HKE 0.25; MV-CLIP 0.43) tend to perceive only the objects themselves and cannot establish such an implicit association, while FIRM explicitly models the spatial and behavioral relations among objects on a bipartite graph through RCR, making the contradiction between "damage" and "respectful" visible and producing a probability of 0.92.

Taken together, these four cases reveal the strengths of FIRM over the baselines from four complementary perspectives—local visual anomaly, spatial-relation conflict, fine-grained sentiment alignment, and implicit behavior–object reasoning—and confirm that FGCA, ICM, and RCR play mutually supportive roles under different sarcasm mechanisms.

