Although our model performs well on samples with explicit cross-modal incongruity, it still shows clear limitations when sarcasm relies on implicit context or external knowledge. We select five representative failure cases (Figure~\ref{fig:4}) from the MMSD2.0 test set and group them into three typical failure patterns: implicit context and external-knowledge dependence, weak text-image correlation, and image-embedded-text dominance.

The first type of failure arises when the sarcastic meaning is primarily driven by information beyond the image and the text, leaving the model unable to establish reliable cross-modal anchors. As shown in Figure~\ref{fig:4-a}, the text "oh, good. now no one will know we're here." carries an obvious ironic tone, yet the image merely depicts three people talking or confronting each other indoors, without any salient local anomaly or sentiment reversal, so its meaning depends heavily on character relations and scene setting. Figure~\ref{fig:4-b} pushes this difficulty further to the event-background level, where the text "wow! what a twist never saw that coming" does not specify the event behind the "twist", and the image only shows a man in a wrestling ring, providing little visual evidence for the alleged reversal—its interpretation thus relies on character identity and dynamic plot development. The same pattern also appears in Figure~\ref{fig:4-c}, where the text "shotgun-axe, hell yeah! mack's got one sweet weapon..." is highly consistent with the image of a "shotgun-axe" hybrid weapon at the surface level and exhibits no obvious conflict, while the irony of "sweet weapon" actually builds on a cultural reference to related TV or movie content. Since FIRM relies solely on the image-text input for fine-grained alignment and relational reasoning and does not incorporate any external knowledge, it tends to fail on all three samples that depend on implicit context or background.

The second type of failure occurs when the text and the image describe essentially different objects or events. As illustrated in Figure~\ref{fig:4-d}, the text "wait, a raid started up outside of a gym and didn't require a pass. so confused; please advise." mainly discusses the "raid" and "gym" mechanism in Pokemon Go, whereas the corresponding image is only a normal Pokemon-catching interface containing no visual element related to either a raid or a gym. Under such a structural mismatch, the model cannot build reliable visual anchors for the key textual concepts, and neither fine-grained alignment nor relational reasoning can effectively take effect.

The third type of failure is somewhat orthogonal and stems from sarcastic cues being concentrated in the text embedded within the image. In Figure~\ref{fig:4-e}, the caption is simply "be different.", which itself carries little sarcastic trigger, while the image contains a long embedded quote, "THEY LAUGH AT ME BECAUSE I'M DIFFERENT. I LAUGH AT THEM BECAUSE THEY'RE ALL THE SAME.", from which the actual sarcastic meaning truly originates. Since FIRM mainly performs token-patch alignment in the visual feature space and does not explicitly model image-embedded text, this critical cue cannot be fully exploited. Forced to rely on the very short caption together with global visual features, the model is naturally prone to incorrect predictions.

Taken together, these cases reveal three main limitations of our method: for sarcasm that depends on implicit context or external knowledge, the model lacks both stable visual anchors and external knowledge sources; for samples with a structural mismatch between text and image, cross-modal alignment is difficult to establish; and for samples dominated by image-embedded text, the absence of explicit OCR-based modeling becomes a critical bottleneck. Future work may further improve the model along the directions of integrating external knowledge bases, enhancing image-text reading ability, and strengthening contextual modeling, in order to better handle deeper pragmatic sarcasm.


\section{Conclusions}
This paper addresses the inadequacy of modeling cross-modal semantic inconsistencies in multimodal sarcasm detection tasks by proposing a multi-branch collaborative framework that integrates fine-grained alignment, conflict mining, and relational reasoning. To overcome the limitations of existing methods—which primarily rely on global feature fusion and struggle to capture implicit sarcasm signals—the model structure is constructed across three layers: “alignment-conflict-reasoning,” enabling hierarchical modeling of cross-modal semantic relationships.

Specifically, it first enhances local semantic correspondences between text and images through a fine-grained cross-modal alignment mechanism. Next, it explicitly models latent semantic inconsistencies via a conflict-aware mining module. Finally, a relationship inference module structurally propagates and integrates key interaction information, thereby enhancing the model's ability to discern complex ironic expressions.

Experimental results on the MMSD 1.0 and MMSD 2.0 datasets demonstrate that the proposed method achieves competitive performance on metrics such as Accuracy and F1-score. Ablation studies further validate the necessity of each module within the overall framework, while parameter sensitivity analysis proves the stability and robustness of the model architecture.

In summary, our method effectively models fine-grained semantic inconsistencies and structural relationships in multimodal sarcasm, offering a viable approach for cross-modal semantic conflict modeling.

